## Remaining gates

- Connect the built-in OmniRoute Radar client to `radar.omniroute.online`,
  verify opt-in and successful catalog sync, and confirm caches populate before
  running the real-catalog report. The current production snapshot has
  `RADAR_ENABLED` absent (default off), all `RADAR_*` overrides absent (client
  fallback is `radar.omniroute.online`), and four Radar caches empty. Public
  community-catalog access is keyless. The user will add the supporter Intel
  key through the dashboard after deployment. Bands remain on the approved
  Arena/manual score sources; Radar Intel rankings are not fed into
  `getTaskFitnessWithSource`.
- After a successful Radar/catalog sync, run the calibration report against
  the real production model catalog; record rated-source coverage, structured-
  output coverage, and candidate counts for every Hermes channel. The local
  isolated-DB CLI smoke is not production evidence. Keep manual ranges for a
  week before considering automatic recalibration.
- Validate band chat routing and full-ID `call_logs.combo_name` in a safe live
  request. Check production catalog/usage ID matches and aggregate-read cost;
  confirm candidate pools are non-empty and
  `OMNIROUTE_AUTO_FREE_FALLBACK_TO_FULL_POOL` is unset.
- Before production reserve enforcement, inspect shared daily metadata shape,
  measure refresh/read cost, and compare proposed exclusions with observed
  quota exhaustion using approved read-only validation. Keep disabled reserve
  as a full stop.
- Production uses the reviewed stock Node 26 Dockerfile; `package.json`
  supports `>=24 <27`. Node 24 portability remains a separate unchecked
  OpenSpec baseline, not a production-runtime blocker. Two builds of staged
  target `db18a17c374506941d10fbc58132eb108d1ea5db` failed. Attempt 1
  (02:57:04–03:08:36 UTC) used a 7-GiB total RAM/swap bound, cpuset 0, and pids
  512; webpack step 19 exited 1 and builder inspection reported `OOM=true`.
  Attempt 2 (03:26:10–03:34:11 UTC) used heap 4096 MiB with the same bounds and
  cached dependencies; it exited 1 on V8 heap exhaustion near 4066 MB and
  `SIGABRT`, with no new cgroup OOM event. Logs and diagnostics were retained;
  neither attempt produced an image or caused migration/cutover. A third
  diagnostic run (03:49:15–03:58:53 UTC) exited 1 as `CANCELED` /
  `context canceled`, not OOM. The watcher expected an exact 9-GiB container
  value, misclassified an approved 9.5-GiB same-container update as a builder
  replacement, then canceled the build. Logs were retained; no image or
  production DB/service changes resulted. Retry 4 ran 04:03:26–04:12:29 UTC
  after preflight reported 27,403,431,936 bytes free disk and 15,284,174,848
  bytes `MemAvailable`. The watcher dry run verified the exact container ID,
  limits, source, and digests; the container had the approved 10-GiB total
  memory-plus-swap limit from startup. It used heap 6144 MiB, two workers,
  webpack, cpuset 0, pids 512, and a 900-second timeout. It exited 1 after a
  cgroup OOM event at 04:12:22 UTC; the Next worker was SIGKILLed at 535 seconds
  and builder memory peaked at 9.999/10 GiB. Kernel evidence showed one Node
  MainThread at 10,220,928 KiB anonymous RSS and 0 file RSS; cgroup memory was
  10,665,197,568 bytes anonymous and about 1 MiB file-backed. This indicates
  anonymous Node memory pressure, with small parent processes rather than
  multiple heavy workers or file-cache pressure. Read-only local configuration
  diagnosis found `webpackBuildWorker=1`, `webpackMemoryOptimizations=true`,
  and Next filesystem cache `maxMemoryGenerations=Infinity`; CPU/page-worker
  settings are already minimal. A candidate `maxMemoryGenerations=0` cap would
  retain disk cache and full UI behavior but may reduce build speed; it is not
  implemented or tested. The user decision whether this policy applies to all
  production builds or is opt-in remains pending; do not amend the spec or
  implement before it is resolved. Afterward, run syntax/native/runtime checks,
  a full 10-GiB, CPU 1, 15-minute exact-SHA build, and isolated runtime-copy/
  migration acceptance. No fifth build is approved or launched. No image,
  start, cutover, or migration resulted. The earlier isolated 1.119-GB
  npm download cache was removed; the 4.242-GB cache layer was preserved and
  reused. Production remains at `f2bddef27ed0807dd5a5e2712bc26536edda8138`.
  Resource readiness and deployment remain open.
- SystemOne live gates remain: confirm OpenRouter request/response shape;
  test Laya-only RU/UK, Laya stop/fallback, and 413/422 recovery followed by a
  small request; collect 50-request direct/proxy latency; verify allowed-model
  policy and production flag-off; monitor deployment variables, rollback, and
  error rows. Laya deployment and the Hermes caller switch are outside this
  repository.
- The user explicitly authorized deployment, production migration, and
  activation. The production source/container is at
  `f2bddef27ed0807dd5a5e2712bc26536edda8138`; the staged deployment target is
  `db18a17c374506941d10fbc58132eb108d1ea5db` and is not live. Deployment is
  pending a clean supported-runtime build/start. Then run the rehearsed
  one-time migration-ledger repair
  against a fresh consistent backup and verify integrity, idempotence, and
